# Every classification the queries make, declared. One row per classifying relation: the relation,
# the type its classes belong to, and what one counted row of it is.
#
# `subject` is what a count counts, declared because nothing else records it
#   A census counts rows, and a row of `arithmetic/all.sqlc` is not the same kind of thing as a row
#   of `checks/fit_axes.sqlc`. The relations here count different things, so a number read across
#   them means nothing, and a threshold set across them would be wrong for most of them. The column
#   is `checks/roster.sqlc`'s, the same word for the same job, which `examples/soundness` already
#   holds for rules.
#
# The relation is not read from the queries
#   A roster is never read from what it governs, so this is a `VALUES` literal like every other
#   roster here. Read the names off `epistemics/class_cells.sqlc` and this file could never disagree
#   with it, and `algebra/class_domain.sqlc` would be anti-joining a set against itself.
#
# `filed` says whether a counted row is something a document filed
#   Most of these relations classify rows somebody filed, so restricting them to a set of documents
#   is a real question, and reports/class_census.sqlc asks it. `checks/fit_axes.sqlc` and
#   `checks/fit_domain.sqlc` do not: the rules here are not filed by anybody. Their classes are the
#   same under every scope, so a scoped report leaves them out rather than print a zero that reads
#   like a finding. algebra/class_domain.sqlc holds the flag against whether any counted row of that
#   relation carries a filing, so the two cannot drift.
#
# `codomain` is the type's bare name, never a rendered one
#   `pg_typeof(x)::text` spells a type through the session's `search_path`, so the same class prints
#   `pm.fit` to one client and `fit` to another, and a literal here would be right in one session
#   and wrong in the next. epistemics/class_cells.sqlc reads `pg_type.typname` instead, which is the
#   name assets/ddl/schema.ddl wrote. `algebra/class_domain.sqlc` compares the two, so a type
#   renamed in the DDL fails here rather than silently classifying nothing.
-- every classification, its type, what each counts, and whether it is filed.
SELECT * FROM (VALUES
  ('arithmetic/all.sqlc',              'arithmetic_verdict', 'computation', true),
  ('layers/remainder.sqlc',            'fit',                'layer',       true),
  ('layers/remainder_scope.sqlc',      'remainder_standing', 'layer',       true),
  ('layers/exposure_scope.sqlc',       'exposure_standing',  'layer',       true),
  ('layers/quantities.sqlc',           'layer_quantity',     'quantity',    true),
  ('composition/part_references.sqlc', 'factor_state',       'part',        true),
  ('checks/fit_axes.sqlc',             'fit_axis',           'rule',        false),
  ('checks/fit_domain.sqlc',           'fit_standing',       'rule cell',   false)
) AS s(relation, codomain, subject, filed)
